WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.167
A brief now stays current, and a
fact is only as strong as its source.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:07.100
Now the same standard for automation.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.100
See what a follow-up would have
done last month, with zero writes,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.400
and only then switch it on.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:16.600
By the end you'll simulate a
play and read what it earned.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.867
You'll pick the next one from the catalog,

00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:22.100
and ask Studio for a
new one in a sentence.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.367
Leo switches on a follow up automation.

00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:31.000
A big import lands, and by morning
every contact has a task and a draft.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.733
In most tools, the first
rehearsal is production.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.700
A play is a declarative automation:
a trigger, a filter, and steps.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.667
Each step is an operation,
one of the typed,

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.767
authorized actions your
console and agent already use.

00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.700
That's the core guarantee:

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.467
a play can never do what a
person in that role couldn't,

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:52.367
and your guardrails apply to every step.

00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:55.667
A trigger is an event, a
schedule, or a segment.

00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:00.333
Open a play in Engines, Plays to
see its trigger, filter and steps.

00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:05.000
Schedule plays fan out over an audience,
like every deal stuck in a stage.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.400
Twenty five run at once by
default, one per member per spell.

00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:12.433
Here's the rule that protects your data.

00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.033
Event plays never fire on bulk writes.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:19.700
An import or a sync can't set off
a flood of follow ups at once.

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:23.167
Step one: simulate first, so you
see the effect before it exists.

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.800
It replays the last thirty
days of trigger history.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.367
You see the matches, what the filter
removed, the actions it would take,

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:32.933
what the gates and compliance
rules would block, and the cost.

00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:37.367
It writes nothing. In the console:
Engines, Plays, choose the play, Dry run.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.900
In the terminal: gtm play
simulate, with the play key.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.967
Or ask your agent: "Simulate
speed to lead over thirty days".

00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:46.300
Power habit: simulate before
and after you edit the filter,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.567
and compare the two.

00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:51.000
Step two: promote it, so a
person owns the decision.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.800
A second person signs the
play like any definition,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.933
and promotion turns it on and says so.
Steps that send are still proposals,

00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:00.733
so a different person approves each send.

00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:05.400
An operation can also tell a play
to stop, with the reason recorded.

00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:08.733
Step three: read the results, so
you judge a play by what it earned.

00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:12.967
In the console: Engines, Plays, Results,
over seven, thirty or ninety days.

00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:13.900
Or ask your agent:

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.800
"What did the renewal runway
play produce over ninety days".

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.333
You'll see runs, outcomes, credited
revenue, spend, a funnel by step,

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:21.567
and why runs were skipped.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:25.667
The honesty rule: a rate built on
fewer than five cases shows no number,

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.200
and says why, instead of
a flattering percentage.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.567
Fixture runs are never counted.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.767
Every approval, ledger row and spend
carries its play, run and step.

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:41.100
So you can trace money to the
exact step in Operate, Runs.

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:42.800
Gotcha on spend:

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.000
a per run budget only bites once a

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.167
play scope cost budget
exists in the ledger,

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.800
so set that first.

00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:54.033
Step four: choose what to build next.

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.700
The catalog scores every blueprint play,

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.633
configured play and open
request on impact and ease,

00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:02.267
and explains each score factor by
factor. Impact weighs reach and fit.

00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:04.867
Ease weighs whether your data
and connections are ready.

00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:07.933
In the console: Studio, plays
catalog, and open a row.

00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:11.567
Or ask your agent: "Which plays
should we turn on first, and why".

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:13.433
Owners and admins can tune the weights.

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:15.133
Blueprints ship ready plays.

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:19.167
Speed to lead routes a form without
taking the account from its owner,

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:20.500
and skips customers.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.767
It creates a fifteen minute first
touch task and drafts a proposed send.

00:03:23.767 --> 00:03:25.733
Meeting prep builds a brief and a task.

00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:27.900
The stage stuck nudge fires when an open

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.067
deal has sat twenty one days or more.

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:35.033
Renewal runway creates tasks
ninety, sixty and thirty days out,

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.700
and drafts a check in at thirty.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.267
Champion change creates a
task to find the new champion.

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:45.033
A typical week: apply a blueprint,
and its plays arrive in dev.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:46.367
Simulate the ones you want.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:50.333
Promote the ones that hold up,
with a second person signing.

00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:51.533
Read the results on Friday.

00:03:51.533 --> 00:03:55.800
Mia owns the choices, Leo the wiring,
and Orbit the repetitive part,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.000
under your rules.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:04:01.267
What if the play you want isn't
in the catalog? That's Studio.

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:06.767
You describe a play or an app in
one sentence, and GTM OS drafts it,

00:04:06.767 --> 00:04:07.500
behind approval.

00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:10.200
In the console: Studio,
and type the sentence.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.033
Or ask your agent in the same
words: "Ask Studio for a play:

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.833
when a deal sits three weeks in one stage,

00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:19.833
nudge the owner and draft
a note to the buyer".

00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:22.533
Studio starts from the
closest catalog play.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:26.333
It resolves references against
your definitions and connections,

00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:28.133
and lists the data needed, the approvers,

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:30.400
a size estimate and its trust mode.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.233
Anything it can't know
becomes a numbered ask.

00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:36.033
With no model route for
extraction, the draft says so,

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:38.333
instead of pretending it was refined.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.100
Answer the asks, then submit. In the
console: Studio, Submit for approval.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.067
Or ask your agent: "Submit the
stage nudge request for approval".

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:48.833
A different person approves,
and the play lands in dev.

00:04:48.833 --> 00:04:51.233
Reaching production still
takes signing and promotion,

00:04:51.233 --> 00:04:54.900
and shipping a play never
turns an engine live.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.900
A rejected or expired approval
returns as changes requested.

00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:02.633
Here's the doubt: an automation
touching my CRM while I'm asleep?

00:05:02.633 --> 00:05:06.167
Every step is an operation,
so a role's limits apply.

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.467
Event plays ignore bulk writes.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:12.767
Sends stay proposals that a
different person approves.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:18.133
Two honest limits. Holdouts, which would
keep a control group out of a play,

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:22.767
aren't shipped yet. Neither are play
libraries for account based programs,

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:25.100
lifecycle and routing service levels.

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:30.100
Quick pass: Simulate first, a person
signs, and the results decide.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.333
The catalog tells you what to build next,

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.600
and Studio turns a
sentence into a request.

00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:40.133
So you can test a play
before it touches anyone,

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:44.967
then ask for the next one in a
sentence. Run one simulation today.

00:05:44.967 --> 00:05:49.900
Up next, how the engines work together:
one loop, from signal to outcome.
